No. update() does not create the representation. The Rep object is created once in the
constructors with setElement(new KickerRep(name)); update() only transfers the current
attribute values onto it. The body has three parts, in this order:

1. Call OpalElement::update() first. The base class handles what all elements share:
   aperture, placement (ELEMEDGE or X/Y/Z/THETA/PHI/PSI) and misalignment.
2. Fetch the existing representation with getElement(), cast it to the concrete type,
   set the element length on its geometry and call its setters.
3. Finish with OpalElement::updateUnknown(rep), which passes on the attributes that are
   not known to OPALX.

void OpalKicker::update() {
    OpalElement::update();

    KickerRep* kick = dynamic_cast<KickerRep*>(getElement());

    kick->getGeometry().setElementLength(Attributes::getReal(itsAttr[LENGTH]));
    kick->setKick(Attributes::getReal(itsAttr[KICK]));
    kick->setFieldMapFN(Attributes::getString(itsAttr[FMAPFN]));

    // Transmit "unknown" attributes.
    OpalElement::updateUnknown(kick);
}

Pitfalls:
- Without the base-class call the element has no aperture and no placement.
- update() is called on all defined objects before an action command executes, so it can
  run more than once for the same element. It must only copy values and be safe to
  repeat.

