examples.mcp_science_output:
No written style guide for headers turned up in the science MCP's index. The convention below comes from the headers it returned, which all follow the same pattern.

## Convention

**1. File header: a `//` comment block at the very top of the file.**
- It starts with an optional description of the class or namespace: a line like `// Class OpalData` or `// Namespace Configure`, followed by indented lines explaining what it does.
- Then the PSI copyright line and the GPLv3 notice.
- The block opens and closes with a bare `//` line.

```cpp
//
// Class MyThing
//   One- or few-line description of what the class does.
//
// Copyright (c) 200x - 2020, Paul Scherrer Institut, Villigen PSI, Switzerland
// All rights reserved
//
// This file is part of OPAL.
//
// OPAL is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
// it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
// the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
// (at your option) any later version.
//
// You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
// along with OPAL. If not, see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
//
#ifndef OPAL_MyThing_HH
#define OPAL_MyThing_HH

...

#endif  // OPAL_MyThing_HH
```

**2. Include guard: a classic `#ifndef`/`#define`/`#endif` guard named `OPAL_<Name>_HH`, not `#pragma once`.**
- The `#endif` repeats the macro name in a trailing comment.
- The notice still says "part of OPAL", even in OPALX.

## Evidence (opalx repo, commit `f9d7679` / `32e8470`)

- **`src/OpalConfigure/Configure.h:1-34`**
  - Lines 1–10: a `// Namespace Configure` description block.
  - Line 12: `Copyright (c) 200x - 2020, Paul Scherrer Institut, Villigen PSI, Switzerland`.
  - Lines 13–24: the GPLv3 text.
  - Lines 25–26 and 34: `#ifndef OPAL_Configure_HH` / `#define OPAL_Configure_HH` / `#endif  // OPAL_Configure_HH`.
- **`src/AbstractObjects/OpalData.h:1-277`**
  - Lines 1–21: a `// Class OpalData` block with the same copyright (`200x - 2021`) and GPL text.
  - Lines 22–23 and 277: the guard `OPAL_OpalData_HH`, closed with `#endif  // OPAL_OpalData_HH`.
- **`src/Elements/OpalPolynomialTimeDependence.h:1-65`**
  - A newer file with no description block, only `// Copyright (c) 2026, Paul Scherrer Institute, ...` plus the GPL text (lines 1–14).
  - A blank line, then the guard `OPAL_OPALPOLYNOMIALTIMEDEPENDENCE_HH` (lines 16–17 and 65).

## Caveats

- **Guard casing isn't consistent.** Older files keep the class name's CamelCase (`OPAL_OpalData_HH`); the newer file uses all caps. The `OPAL_` prefix and `_HH` suffix are the stable parts. Matching the files near yours is the safest choice.
- **Don't copy wording from the newer file.** It has "Paul Scherrer Institute" instead of "Institut", and "GNU General License" (missing "Public") on line 12. The older files have the canonical text.
- **Headers use `.h`.** One exception exists (`Quaternion.hpp` in `src/Algorithms/CMakeLists.txt`), but `.h` is the norm.
- **Different commit.** The index is at OPALX commits `f9d7679`/`32e8470`, not the commit recorded in `opalx_examples.json`, so line numbers may differ from your local checkout.

Separately, the claude.ai Google Drive connector needs authorization, which you can do in your claude.ai connector settings. It wasn't needed for this answer.

