## Answer

**No, this is not GPU-safe.** The kernel reads `strength_m` and `getGeometry()` through `this`, and on a GPU build that pointer refers to host memory.

### Why it breaks

`KOKKOS_LAMBDA` captures by value (`[=]`). When the body uses a member such as `strength_m`, or calls a member function such as `getGeometry()`, the lambda copies the `this` pointer. It does not copy the values themselves. On a CUDA or HIP build the kernel then dereferences a host pointer on the device. There are two problems in the snippet:

1. **`getGeometry().getElementLength()` inside the kernel.** This is a host member function and is not marked `KOKKOS_FUNCTION`. With CUDA it either fails to compile ("calling a `__host__` function from a `__device__` function") or reads `this` on the device. It also needlessly recomputes the same value for every particle.
2. **`strength_m` inside the kernel.** This is really `this->strength_m`. It compiles, but on the device it reads through a host pointer, which gives an illegal memory access or garbage values.

The views (`Rview`, `Bview`) and `pc->getLocalNum()` are fine. Views are meant to be copied into kernels, and `getLocalNum()` is called on the host before the launch.

### How OPALX handles this

The existing elements copy every member they need into a local variable on the host first, then capture only those locals:

- `src/AbsBeamline/Multipole.cpp:241–273` (commits `32e8470`/`ca79921`; lines 245–278 at `f9d7679`). It sets `double elemLength = getGeometry().getElementLength();` under the comment "Local variables that are copied into the kernel". It then copies `NormalComponents`, `SkewComponents`, `max_NormalComponent_m` and `max_SkewComponent_m` into locals under the comment "Capture member variables by value for the kernel". The bounds check in the kernel is `Rview(i)(2) > 0 && Rview(i)(2) <= elemLength`, the same check your element uses.
- `src/AbsBeamline/VariableRFCavity.cpp:114–130` follows the same pattern: `const auto halfWidth = halfWidth_m;` and `const auto length = getGeometry().getElementLength();` are set before `Kokkos::parallel_for`.

### Fixed version

```cpp
void MyKicker::apply(const std::shared_ptr<ParticleContainer_t>& pc) {
    auto Rview          = pc->R.getView();
    auto Bview          = pc->B.getView();
    const size_t nLocal = pc->getLocalNum();

    // Local variables that are copied into the kernel (no `this` on the device)
    const double elemLength = getGeometry().getElementLength();
    const double strength   = strength_m;

    Kokkos::parallel_for(
            "MyKicker::apply", nLocal, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const size_t i) {
                if (Rview(i)(2) > 0 && Rview(i)(2) <= elemLength) {
                    Bview(i)(1) += strength;
                }
            });
}
```

### Other things to check

- **Return type.** At commit `f9d7679` the index shows `bool Multipole::apply(...)` and `bool VariableRFCavity::apply(...)`, both ending in `return false;`. The older commits use `void`. Match whatever base-class signature your checkout has.
- **Helper functions.** If the kernel ever calls a helper, it must be device-callable (`KOKKOS_INLINE_FUNCTION`/`KOKKOS_FUNCTION`), and you should pass it the captured locals rather than members. That is how `Multipole` passes `normalComponents`, `skewComponents` and the other locals into `computeField`.
- **Stricter check.** If you want the compiler to catch this, use `KOKKOS_CLASS_LAMBDA` only when you really mean to capture `*this` by copy. Otherwise, keep the kernels free of any member access.

The science MCP indexes several OPALX commits that differ from each other, so the line numbers above may not match your checkout exactly.

