No, this isn't the right way. It only works by accident in a host-only build, such as `PLATFORMS=SERIAL` or OpenMP.

## What's wrong

`pc->R.getView()` and `pc->P.getView()` return the **device** `Kokkos::View`s of the particle attributes. With a GPU backend (CUDA/HIP), the data lives in device memory. Indexing those views with `Rview(i)` in a plain host `for` loop then either crashes with an illegal memory access or fails a Kokkos access check. OPALX never reads `R`/`P` on the host this way. It always copies them into a host view first. A minor point: OPALX code typically follows the device→host copy with a `Kokkos::fence()`.

## How OPALX does it

`H5PartWrapperForPT::writeStepData` in `src/Structure/H5PartWrapperForPT.cpp:429-624` (OPALX, commits `32e8470`/`ca79921`/`f9d7679` in the science index) does this:

```cpp
auto rViewDevice = pc->R.getView();
auto rView       = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(rViewDevice);
copyToHost(rView, rViewDevice);   // Kokkos::fence(); Kokkos::deep_copy(host, dev); Kokkos::fence();
...
for (size_t i = 0; i < numLocalParticles; ++i)
    f64buffer[i] = rView(i)(0);
```

It uses the same pattern for `P`, `ID`, `Bin`, `Pol`, `E` and `B`. Other host-side code in OPALX handles the copy in one of two ways:
- **One call:** `Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->P.getView())`, for example in `unit_tests/PartBunch/TestParticleContainer.cpp` (`CreateParticles_GrowPreservesExistingData`, ~l. 495–547).
- **IPPL helper:** `ParticleAttrib::getHostMirror()` (IPPL `src/Particle/ParticleAttrib.h:150`, `return Kokkos::create_mirror(getView());`) followed by an explicit `Kokkos::deep_copy`, as in `unit_tests/PartBunch/Binning/TestBinning.cpp:152-174`. Note that `getHostMirror()` always allocates a new buffer and does **not** copy any data. The `deep_copy` is required.

## Fixed version

```cpp
auto pc = /* particle container */;
const size_t nLocal = pc->getLocalNum();

Kokkos::fence();  // make sure pending device kernels writing R/P have finished
auto Rhost = Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->R.getView());
auto Phost = Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->P.getView());

for (size_t i = 0; i < nLocal; ++i) {
    out << Rhost(i)[0] << " " << Rhost(i)[1] << " " << Rhost(i)[2] << " "
        << Phost(i)[2] << "\n";
}
```

The OPALX writer style is equivalent:

```cpp
auto Rdev  = pc->R.getView();
auto Rhost = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(Rdev);
Kokkos::fence();
Kokkos::deep_copy(Rhost, Rdev);
// same for P
```

A few other details:
- `create_mirror_view`/`create_mirror_view_and_copy` don't allocate or copy when the view is already host-accessible, so the serial build costs nothing extra. `getHostMirror()` (`create_mirror`) always allocates.
- Loop over `getLocalNum()`, not the view extent. The attribute buffers can be larger than the number of live particles: the test checks `pc->R.size() >= getLocalNum()` after a grow.
- Each MPI rank only sees its own local particles. Writing all particles means each rank writes its share (as the H5Part writer does) or you gather them.
- Use `getQView()` for charge rather than reading `Q` directly. Depending on `getQMStorageMode()`, charge is stored per particle or as a single value at index 0 (`H5PartWrapperForPT.cpp`, `q` block).

**Caveat:** the science MCP indexes OPALX at commits `32e8470`/`ca79921`/`f9d7679`, which are not necessarily the commit your dataset uses. Line numbers may differ slightly in your checkout.

