No. Inside a `KOKKOS_LAMBDA`, OPALX uses the `Kokkos::` math functions (`Kokkos::sqrt`, `Kokkos::sin`, `Kokkos::cos`, …), not `std::`. `std::sin`/`std::cos`/`std::sqrt` are host functions, so they aren't guaranteed to compile or run inside device code (CUDA/HIP builds). The `Kokkos::` versions work on every backend. The physics in your kernel is fine; only the namespace is wrong.

**Fixed kernel:**

```cpp
auto Rview = pc->R.getView();
auto Pview = pc->P.getView();
Kokkos::parallel_for(
        "rotateByPhase", pc->getLocalNum(), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const size_t i) {
            const double gamma = Kokkos::sqrt(1.0 + dot(Pview(i), Pview(i)));
            const double phi   = k * Rview(i)[2] / gamma;
            const double cphi  = Kokkos::cos(phi);
            const double sphi  = Kokkos::sin(phi);
            const double px    = Pview(i)[0];
            const double py    = Pview(i)[1];
            Pview(i)[0]        = px * cphi - py * sphi;
            Pview(i)[1]        = px * sphi + py * cphi;
        });
```

I also stored `cos(phi)` and `sin(phi)` in variables so each is computed once. And I saved `py` next to `px`, so it's obvious that both rotated components use the old momentum. Your version was already correct on that point, but only because `[1]` happened to be read before it was overwritten.

**Where this comes from (science MCP, OPALX repo):**
- `src/Distribution/OpalFlatTop.cpp:349–378` (indexed commits `32e8470`, `f9d7679`, `ca79921`): a particle kernel built like yours. It uses `Kokkos::sqrt`, `Kokkos::acos`, `Kokkos::sin`, `Kokkos::cos` and `Kokkos::fabs` throughout. It computes gamma as `Kokkos::sqrt(1.0 + p[0]*p[0] + p[1]*p[1] + p[2]*p[2])`.
- `src/Distribution/Uniform.cpp:59–73`: `Kokkos::pow`, `Kokkos::sqrt`, `Kokkos::cos`, `Kokkos::sin` inside `KOKKOS_LAMBDA`.
- `src/Algorithms/DistributionMoments.cpp:619–625`: `double gamma0 = Kokkos::sqrt(dot(Pview(k)) + 1.0);`, plus `Kokkos::pow`. So calling the IPPL vector `dot` on `Pview(...)` inside a kernel is normal OPALX style. That file uses the one-argument form `dot(Pview(k))`. The search didn't show whether OPALX prefers that over `dot(P, P)`, so I left your call as it was.

**Limitations:**
- The MCP index has no written style guide or convention document for this; it returned nothing useful for that search. The answer is based on the pattern that recurs across these kernels.
- The indexed commits may not match your checkout.

The `claude.ai Google Drive` connector also needs to be authorized before it can be used. You can do that in your claude.ai connector settings. This answer didn't need it.

