No, the snippet is wrong. It can hang on multiple ranks, and when the container grows it can wipe the particles that are already there.

## Problems

**1. Returning early when `nNew == 0` can deadlock MPI.**
In IPPL, `ParticleBase::create` always ends with a collective call, even when `nLocal == 0`:

```cpp
if (nLocal > 0) { ... localNum_m += nLocal; }
Comm->allreduce(localNum_m, totalNum_m, 1, std::plus<size_type>());
```
(ippl `src/Particle/ParticleBase.hpp:91-123`)

`nNew` differs per rank. A rank with nothing to emit returns before the `allreduce`, while the other ranks wait in it. OPALX's own emitter has the same early return commented out for this reason:

```cpp
// if (nNew == 0) { return; } // don't return, see why below
...
// Note that createParticles can be safely called with nNew=0 (no-op), but it NEEDS to be
// called, since other ranks might have != 0 leading to a MPI_Allreduce.
pc_m->createParticles(nNew);
```
(OPALX `src/Distribution/FlatTop.cpp:372-412`, `FlatTop::emitParticles`)

**2. Calling `create(nNew)` directly instead of `createParticles(nNew)`.**
When an attribute has to grow, `ParticleAttrib::create` chooses between two paths (ippl `src/Particle/ParticleAttrib.hpp:25-37`):
- with `non_destructive`, it calls `Kokkos::resize`, which keeps the existing entries;
- otherwise it calls `Kokkos::realloc`, which is "destructive (free + alloc, no copy)".

OPALX's wrapper `ParticleContainer::createParticles` (OPALX `src/PartBunch/ParticleContainer.hpp:753-799`) does what emission needs:
- It calls `this->create(numParticles, true)` with `non_destructive = true`.
- It initializes the new slots in `[oldLocalNum, oldLocalNum + n)`: `InvalidMask = false`, and `dt`, `Phi`, `Bin`, `E`, `B` set to 0. Its comment says this is needed because "emitted particles are created after the per-step field reset, so E/B must be explicitly cleared here."
- It warns when the views were reallocated, and suggests raising `BEAM::NALLOC` or the overallocation factor.

Its doc comment also says: "The underlying `create` is a collective call, so all MPI ranks must call this function."

The search results didn't show the default value of `create`'s second parameter. So I can't say whether your `create(nNew)` is destructive. It certainly skips the slot initialization above.

**3. Global bookkeeping (only if you need it).**
`FlatTop` needs the global emitted count, so it adds an `MPI_Allreduce` on `nNew` before updating `totalEmitted_m`. Using only the rank-local count "would make the space-charge mesh rank-dependent." After filling the new particles it also calls `pc_m->markMomentsDirty()`.

## Fixed version

Modelled on `FlatTop::emitParticles`:

```cpp
void MySource::emitParticles(double t, double dt) {
    const size_t nNew = countNewParticles(t, dt);  // differs per rank

    // No early return: createParticles -> ParticleBase::create ends in an
    // MPI allreduce, so every rank must call it, even with nNew == 0.
    const size_t first = pc_m->getLocalNum();
    pc_m->createParticles(nNew);  // non-destructive grow + init of the new slots

    // Only if the source tracks global emission progress:
    // unsigned long localNew = nNew, globalNew = 0;
    // MPI_Allreduce(&localNew, &globalNew, 1, MPI_UNSIGNED_LONG, MPI_SUM,
    //               ippl::Comm->getCommunicator());
    // totalEmitted_m += globalNew;

    if (nNew > 0) {  // local work only, safe to skip
        fillNewParticles(first, nNew, dt);
    }
    pc_m->markMomentsDirty();  // call unconditionally if moments are computed collectively
}
```

