examples.mcp_science_output:
OPALX mesh fields are IPPL fields that live on Kokkos views. To work on every cell, you run a device-parallel loop over the field's index range and index the field, or its view, inside a `KOKKOS_LAMBDA`. The OPALX source (as returned by the science MCP) does this in two ways.

## 1. The field types

`src/Algorithms/PBunchDefs.h` (lines 1–33) defines the mesh field aliases:

```cpp
using Mesh_t = ippl::UniformCartesian<double, 3>;
template <unsigned Dim = 3, class... ViewArgs>
using Field_t  = ippl::Field<double, Dim, ViewArgs...>;                     // scalar field
template <typename T = double, unsigned Dim = 3, class... ViewArgs>
using VField_t = ippl::Field<ippl::Vector<T, Dim>, Dim, ViewArgs...>;      // vector field
```

So a `VField_t` cell holds an `ippl::Vector<T, Dim>`.

## 2. Recommended: dimension-independent loop with `getFieldRangePolicy()` + `ippl::apply`

This is the idiom OPALX itself uses for "do something to every cell".

**Element-wise update**: `src/PartBunch/Solve2d5.hpp`, `scatterToGrid` (lines 178–248, the scaling step near the end, ~lines 240–245):

```cpp
const auto rhoView = rho_m->getView();
...
ippl::parallel_for(
        "Solve2d5::scatterToGrid::scale", rho_m->getFieldRangePolicy(),
        KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const ippl::RangePolicy<Dim>::index_array_type& idx) {
            ippl::apply(rhoView, idx) = ippl::apply(rhoView, idx) / scale;
        });
Kokkos::fence();
```

The source comment there explains that `ippl::apply` "accesses a view using indices in an array like structure and is from IpplOperations.h". This means `idx` is a `Dim`-sized index array, so the same code works for 2D or 3D fields.

**Reduction over a vector field**: `src/PartBunch/Binning/ParallelReduceTools.h`, `vnorm` (lines 357–367):

```cpp
ippl::parallel_reduce(
        "VectorFieldNormReduce", field.getFieldRangePolicy(),
        KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const ippl::RangePolicy<Dim>::index_array_type& idx, T& loc_sum) {
            ippl::Vector<T, Dim> e = apply(field, idx);
            loc_sum += std::pow(e.dot(e), p / 2.0);
        },
        Kokkos::Sum<T>(sum));
```

Note that `apply` works on the field object itself as well as on its view, and it returns an `ippl::Vector` for a `VField_t`.

### Applied to your case: combining two vector fields cell by cell

Following the same pattern (this is my composition, not a function that exists in OPALX):

```cpp
// A, B, C: VField_t<double, 3> on the same FieldLayout/mesh (same nghost)
auto aView = A.getView();
auto bView = B.getView();
auto cView = C.getView();
const double alpha = ..., beta = ...;

ippl::parallel_for(
        "combineVFields", C.getFieldRangePolicy(),
        KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const ippl::RangePolicy<3>::index_array_type& idx) {
            ippl::Vector<double, 3> a = ippl::apply(aView, idx);
            ippl::Vector<double, 3> b = ippl::apply(bView, idx);
            ippl::apply(cView, idx)   = alpha * a + beta * b;   // or a cross/dot/component-wise op
        });
Kokkos::fence();
```

Things to keep in mind:
- **Capture views by value.** Before the kernel, take each view out with `getView()`, or a plain `auto` copy of it, as `scatterToGrid` does with `rhoView`. Do not capture class members inside a `KOKKOS_LAMBDA`.
- **Same layout and ghost count.** All fields must share one layout and one ghost count, so that the same `idx` means the same cell in each. `mirrorField` in `src/PartBunch/FieldMirror.hpp` asserts exactly this for its src and dst fields (lines ~49–52: `&src.getLayout() == &dst.getLayout()` and `src.getNghost() == dst.getNghost()`).
- **Fence before host use.** Call `Kokkos::fence()` before you read the result on the host or hand it to a solver. All the OPALX examples above do this.

## 3. Alternative: explicit `Kokkos::MDRangePolicy` on the raw view

Use this when you need explicit `(i, j, k)` indices, a sub-range, or different index maps for source and destination. Here you handle the ghost offset yourself.

**`src/PartBunch/FieldMirror.hpp`, `mirrorField` (lines 45–183)**: it computes local bounds as `global - ldom.first() + nghost` and then runs:

```cpp
using mdrange_type = Kokkos::MDRangePolicy<Kokkos::Rank<3>>;
Kokkos::parallel_for(
        "opalx::mirrorField::self",
        mdrange_type({first0, first1, first2}, {last0, last1, last2}),
        KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const size_t i, const size_t j, const size_t k) {
            size_t idx[3]    = {i, j, k};
            idx[axis_c]      = static_cast<size_t>(k_shift) - idx[axis_c];
            dstView(i, j, k) = srcView(idx[0], idx[1], idx[2]);
        });
Kokkos::fence();
```

**`src/PartBunch/Solve2d5.hpp`, `solvePoissons` (lines 435–498)**: it loops over 2D view extents and accesses vector components with `[c]`, offsetting by `nghost` (obtained from `E_m->getNghost()`):

```cpp
using Policy = Kokkos::MDRangePolicy<Kokkos::Rank<2>>;
auto e3d    = E_m->getView();
auto nghost = E_m->getNghost();
...
Kokkos::parallel_for(
        "Solve2d5::solvePoissons::copy", Policy({0, 0}, {e2d.extent(0), e2d.extent(1)}),
        KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const size_t i, const size_t j) {
            e3d(i, j, z + nghost)[0] = e2d(i, j)[0];
            e3d(i, j, z + nghost)[1] = e2d(i, j)[1];
            e3d(i, j, z + nghost)[2] = 0.0;
        });
```

If you use the view extents (`view.extent(d)`) as bounds, the loop also covers the ghost layers, since the view includes them. To touch only interior cells, use the range `[nghost, extent - nghost)`, or simply use `getFieldRangePolicy()` from section 2.

**`src/Structure/DirichletPlaneWriter.h` (lines 132–142)**: this shows a third variant. It runs a flattened 1D loop over `nx*ny` that recovers `(ii, jj)` from `idx` and adds `nghost` before indexing `fieldView(iLocal, jLocal, k)`.

## Summary

To combine two vector fields cell by cell, take each field's view with `getView()`. Then call `ippl::parallel_for(name, field.getFieldRangePolicy(), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const ippl::RangePolicy<Dim>::index_array_type& idx) {...})`, read and write cells with `ippl::apply(view, idx)` (which gives an `ippl::Vector` for a `VField_t`), and finish with `Kokkos::fence()`. This is the pattern in `Solve2d5.hpp` `scatterToGrid` and `ParallelReduceTools.h` `vnorm`. Use a raw `Kokkos::MDRangePolicy` with manual `nghost` offsets only if you need explicit indices or index remapping, as in `FieldMirror.hpp` and `Solve2d5.hpp` `solvePoissons`.

Caveat: the MCP index did not return the IPPL source for `getFieldRangePolicy()` or `ippl::apply` (they come from IPPL, not OPALX). The description of their behaviour above is therefore inferred from how OPALX calls them, not from their definitions.

