examples.mcp_science_output:
OPALX times code sections with **IPPL's `IpplTimings`** API. You get a named timer, start it, and stop it around the section you want to measure. I found this pattern throughout the sources returned by the science MCP. The answer below relies only on those sources.

## The basic pattern

```cpp
// 1. Get (or create) a named timer. The name is the label in the timing report.
static IpplTimings::TimerRef myTimer = IpplTimings::getTimer("mySection");

// 2. Bracket the code you want to measure.
IpplTimings::startTimer(myTimer);
// ... code to time ...
IpplTimings::stopTimer(myTimer);
```

These calls take a name and a timer reference, so the same name appears to refer to the same timer each time. Calling start/stop repeatedly (for example, once per step) then adds up the time for that section.

## Examples in the code

**1. A local `static` timer inside a function.** This is the easiest option for a one-off measurement. From `src/PartBunch/LoadBalancer.hpp` (lines 54–69, `updateLayout`):

```cpp
static IpplTimings::TimerRef tupdateLayout = IpplTimings::getTimer("updateLayout");
IpplTimings::startTimer(tupdateLayout);
fc_m->updateFieldLayoutsAfterLayoutChange(fs_m ? fs_m->getStype() : "");
pc_m->updateLayout(*fl, *mesh);
IpplTimings::stopTimer(tupdateLayout);

static IpplTimings::TimerRef tupdatePLayout = IpplTimings::getTimer("updatePB");
IpplTimings::startTimer(tupdatePLayout);
pc_m->update();
pc_m->markMomentsDirty();
IpplTimings::stopTimer(tupdatePLayout);
```

The whole program is timed the same way in `src/Main.cpp` (`main`, around lines 145–146):

```cpp
static IpplTimings::TimerRef mainTimer = IpplTimings::getTimer("mainTimer");
IpplTimings::startTimer(mainTimer);
```

**2. A timer stored as a class member.** Use this when a class times several of its own methods.

- Declare the members. From `src/Algorithms/ParallelTracker.h` (the `// --- Timers ---` block, around lines 85–92):
  ```cpp
  IpplTimings::TimerRef timeIntegrationTimer1_m;
  IpplTimings::TimerRef timeIntegrationTimer2_m;
  IpplTimings::TimerRef fieldEvaluationTimer_m;
  ...
  ```
- Initialise them in the constructor. From `src/Algorithms/ParallelTracker.cpp` (lines 70–83):
  ```cpp
  timeIntegrationTimer1_m(IpplTimings::getTimer("TIntegration1")),
  timeIntegrationTimer2_m(IpplTimings::getTimer("TIntegration2")),
  fieldEvaluationTimer_m(IpplTimings::getTimer("External field eval")),
  ```
- Use them in the methods. From `ParallelTracker::timeIntegration2` (`src/Algorithms/ParallelTracker.cpp`, around lines 648–676):
  ```cpp
  IpplTimings::startTimer(timeIntegrationTimer2_m);
  // kick/push particles for every active container ...
  IpplTimings::stopTimer(timeIntegrationTimer2_m);
  ```

`src/PartBunch/Binning/BinHisto.h` does the same thing. It declares `IpplTimings::TimerRef bDeviceSyncronizationT, bHistogramInitT, bMergeBinsT;` (around lines 561–563). It sets them in `initTimers()` (lines 519–523) with `IpplTimings::getTimer("bDeviceSyncronization")` and similar calls. It then brackets `sync()` (lines 342–357) and `initPostSum()` (lines 276–288) with `startTimer`/`stopTimer`.

A timer can also be set in a constructor's initializer list, as in `src/Structure/H5PartWrapper.cpp` (lines 35–37): `h5OpenCloseTimer_m(IpplTimings::getTimer("H5 open close"))`.

## Writing out the results

To dump all the timers to a file, call `IpplTimings::print`. You can pass a map of extra key/value pairs to include in the file. Example from `tools/mslang/mslang.cpp` (lines 122–144):

```cpp
IpplTimings::stopTimer(mainTimer);

std::map<std::string, unsigned int> characteristicValues;
characteristicValues.insert(std::make_pair("method", method));
characteristicValues.insert(std::make_pair("num particles", N));
std::stringstream ss;
ss << "timing__m_=_" << method << "__np_=_" << N << "__nbf_=_" << baseBlocks.size() << ".dat";
IpplTimings::print(ss.str(), characteristicValues);
```

The science MCP did not return the place where the main OPALX executable prints its timers at the end of a run. I can't show where that report is written, only that timers registered with `getTimer` are the ones `IpplTimings` manages.

## Don't use `OPALTimer::Timer` for this

`src/Utilities/Timer.h` (lines 1–55) defines a class called `OPALTimer::Timer`, but it is not a profiler. It saves the system clock time when it is created and only provides `date()` and `time()` strings ("Encapsulates the UNIX wall time functions. Get Calendar date or time."). To measure how long a section takes, use `IpplTimings`.

## Summary

1. Make a timer: `static IpplTimings::TimerRef t = IpplTimings::getTimer("name");`. Use a class member set in the constructor if you want to reuse it across methods.
2. Put `IpplTimings::startTimer(t);` before the section and `IpplTimings::stopTimer(t);` after it.
3. To write the timings to a file, call `IpplTimings::print(filename[, map])`.

