No. Two things are missing.

1. IPPL is never initialised. Anything that creates a Kokkos view or uses ippl::Comm
   needs MPI and Kokkos running. OPALX tests do this once per test suite in a fixture,
   because it cannot be repeated per test:

class MyContainerTest : public ::testing::Test {
protected:
    static void SetUpTestSuite() {
        int argc    = 0;
        char** argv = nullptr;
        ippl::initialize(argc, argv);
    }
    static void TearDownTestSuite() { ippl::finalize(); }
};

2. dtView may be device memory. Copy to the host before the EXPECT macros read it:

TEST_F(MyContainerTest, NewParticlesHaveZeroDt) {
    auto pc = makeContainer();
    pc->createParticles(8);
    auto dtHost = Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->dt.getView());
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
        EXPECT_DOUBLE_EQ(dtHost(i), 0.0);
    }
}

Further points:
- Include "Ippl.h". All views and containers must be destroyed before TearDownTestSuite
  runs, so keep them local to the test or to helper functions.
- A container built by hand needs pc->setBunchStateHandler(
  std::make_shared<BunchStateHandler>()) before moments are used.
- Code under test that logs through the global gmsg needs it set in SetUpTestSuite
  (gmsg = new Inform(nullptr, -1);) and deleted in TearDownTestSuite.
- Register the test with add_opalx_test(); it is started through mpiexec.

examples.mcp_science_output:
No, not quite. The thing being checked is fine: new particles really do start with `dt == 0`. The test harness around it is wrong in two ways, and one of them will crash or give undefined behaviour.

## What the science MCP shows

**1. `createParticles` does set `dt` to zero.**
`src/PartBunch/ParticleContainer.hpp` (lines 754–800, `createParticles`) creates the particles with `this->create(numParticles, true)`. It then runs a `Kokkos::parallel_for` over the new slots that sets `invalid(idx)=false`, `dtView(idx)=0.0`, `Phi=0`, `Bin=0`, `E=0` and `B=0`, followed by `Kokkos::fence()`. So the expectation `dt == 0` for new particles is correct.

**2. OPALX unit tests use a fixture that starts and stops IPPL.**
In `unit_tests/PartBunch/TestParticleContainer.cpp`, the existing tests are all `TEST_F(ParticleContainerTest, ...)`. The fixture does this:
- `static void SetUpTestSuite()` (lines 39–43) calls `ippl::initialize(argc, argv)` with `argc = 0` and `argv = nullptr`.
- `static void TearDownTestSuite()` (line 45) calls `ippl::finalize()`.
- `makeContainer()` (around lines 48–67) is a fixture member. It builds `Mesh_t<3>` and `FieldLayout_t<3>`, then `std::make_shared<PC_t>(mesh, fl)`, then calls `setBunchStateHandler(std::make_shared<BunchStateHandler>())`.

The tests are built with `add_opalx_test(TestParticleContainer USE_GTEST_MAIN)` in `unit_tests/PartBunch/CMakeLists.txt`. With `USE_GTEST_MAIN` there is no custom `main`, so nothing initializes IPPL, Kokkos or MPI unless the fixture does it. (I could not get the body of `add_opalx_test` from the MCP. This reading comes from the flag's name and from every fixture doing its own `ippl::initialize`.)

**3. Existing tests copy device data to the host before reading it.**
They never index `getView()` directly on the host. They always copy first, for example `Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->dt.getView())` in `DtScaling_RoundTrip_SingleValueMode` and `DtScaling_RoundTrip_AttributeMode` (lines 181–237).

**4. Tests set the global Q/M storage mode explicitly.**
Each test sets `Options::useQMAttributes` before calling `makeContainer()`. Examples are `ChargeMass_*` (lines 123–175) and `CreateParticles_AssignsStridedIDs` (lines 611–627). The flag is global and the constructor reads it, so leaving it unset makes the result depend on which tests ran before.

## Problems in the submitted test

| # | Problem | Consequence |
|---|---------|-------------|
| 1 | It uses a plain `TEST`, so no fixture runs and `ippl::initialize()` / `ippl::finalize()` are never called | Building the `FieldLayout`/`ParticleContainer` (MPI communicator, Kokkos views) happens before IPPL, Kokkos and MPI are initialized. That is undefined behaviour or a crash. |
| 2 | It calls a free function `makeContainer()` that doesn't exist | In OPALX, `makeContainer()` is a member of the `ParticleContainerTest` fixture, so it is only reachable from `TEST_F`. |
| 3 | `dtView(i)` reads `pc->dt.getView()` directly on the host | On a CUDA or HIP build this is device memory, so the host access is illegal. You need a host mirror (`create_mirror_view_and_copy`). |
| 4 | `Options::useQMAttributes` is never set | The container's Q/M storage mode depends on global state left by earlier tests. This doesn't change `dt`, but it breaks the suite's convention. |
| 5 | It hard-codes 8 instead of checking `getLocalNum()` | This is minor, but asserting the local count first keeps the loop from running past the particles that actually exist. |

## Fixed version

Add it as a new `TEST_F` inside `unit_tests/PartBunch/TestParticleContainer.cpp`, in the same namespace and scope as the existing tests. It needs nothing new in CMake, because `TestParticleContainer` is already registered with `USE_GTEST_MAIN`.

```cpp
TEST_F(ParticleContainerTest, CreateParticles_NewParticlesHaveZeroDt) {
    Options::useQMAttributes = false;
    auto pc                  = makeContainer();   // fixture member

    constexpr size_t nPart = 8;
    pc->createParticles(nPart);
    ASSERT_EQ(pc->getLocalNum(), nPart);

    // dt lives in device memory: copy to host before reading
    auto dtHost = Kokkos::create_mirror_view_and_copy(Kokkos::HostSpace(), pc->dt.getView());
    for (size_t i = 0; i < nPart; ++i) {
        EXPECT_DOUBLE_EQ(dtHost(i), 0.0) << "particle " << i;
    }
}
```

If you really want it in a separate file, you have to reproduce the fixture there. Then register the file in `unit_tests/PartBunch/CMakeLists.txt`:

```cpp
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include "PartBunch/ParticleContainer.hpp"
// plus the same headers TestParticleContainer.cpp uses for Mesh_t, FieldLayout_t,
// BunchStateHandler and Options

using PC_t = ParticleContainer<double, 3>;

class MyContainerTest : public ::testing::Test {
protected:
    static void SetUpTestSuite() {
        int argc    = 0;
        char** argv = nullptr;
        ippl::initialize(argc, argv);
    }
    static void TearDownTestSuite() { ippl::finalize(); }

    std::shared_ptr<PC_t> makeContainer() {
        ippl::Vector<int, 3> nr        = 8;
        ippl::Vector<double, 3> rmin   = -4.0;
        ippl::Vector<double, 3> rmax   = 4.0;
        ippl::Vector<double, 3> origin = rmin;
        ippl::Vector<double, 3> hr     = (rmax - rmin) / ippl::Vector<double, 3>(nr);
        std::array<bool, 3> decomp     = {true, true, true};

        ippl::NDIndex<3> domain;
        for (unsigned i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            domain[i] = ippl::Index(nr[i]);
        }

        Mesh_t<3> mesh(domain, hr, origin);
        FieldLayout_t<3> fl(MPI_COMM_WORLD, domain, decomp, true);

        auto pc = std::make_shared<PC_t>(mesh, fl);
        pc->setBunchStateHandler(std::make_shared<BunchStateHandler>());
        return pc;
    }
};

TEST_F(MyContainerTest, NewParticlesHaveZeroDt) { /* body as above */ }
```

```cmake
# unit_tests/PartBunch/CMakeLists.txt
add_opalx_test(MyContainerTest USE_GTEST_MAIN)
```

The container in each test is a local `shared_ptr`, so it is destroyed at the end of the test body, before `TearDownTestSuite()` calls `ippl::finalize()`. That ordering matters: no Kokkos views outlive finalize.

**Sources:**
- `src/PartBunch/ParticleContainer.hpp` lines 754–800 (`createParticles` zero-initializes `dt`)
- `unit_tests/PartBunch/TestParticleContainer.cpp`: lines 39–43 and 45 (setup and teardown), around 48–67 (`makeContainer`), 152–175, 181–237 and 611–627 (usage patterns)
- `unit_tests/PartBunch/CMakeLists.txt` (`add_opalx_test(TestParticleContainer USE_GTEST_MAIN)`)

